{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794244691,"nonce":"obPwJa4rqe21Xll5wSrd9i2zQKk=","title":"An animating flow shader keeps the CPU running the whole frame loop","message":"Reported from the russl toy: while the live preview (a canvas with an\nanimating @flow shader) runs, the CPU stays awake for every frame, not only the\nGPU.\n\n`blinc_app.has_active_flows()` sets `flow_needs_redraw`, which keeps the\nwindowed pacer at vsync (or the fps cap). Each of those frames runs the full\nframe path: the event/state prelude, the stylesheet state gate, CSS animation\nand transition ticks, the compositor fast path or repaint, and command\nencoding, even when nothing but the shader's time uniform changed.\n\nTo find out: profile the toy idle with the preview running (Instruments Time\nProfiler / xctrace) and see what the CPU spends per frame. Candidates: a\nflow-only frame that skips the UI passes and only re-encodes the canvas layer\nwith the new time; and whether the static UI is re-painted or blitted from the\ncompositor cache on those frames.","files":null}]}